    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-1803",
      "problem": "Plot the graph of $y=\\left( \\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x.$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph of $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^x$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x<0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0.$\n\nWhen $x=-1,$ we have $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{-1} =4.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $\\left( -1, 4\\right).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194784/assets/4cbb5007cf380afe964bea8b5970e4a37924f38401ec24cb856b02c0510676eb.png)",
